---
.title = "What you can depend on",
.description = "The checks behind glowcap's protocol, transports, bindings, and platforms.",
.date = .date("2026-07-25T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

glowcap has no stable release yet. Pin a commit, and treat the API as able to
move. None of the checks below is a compatibility or security promise.

Three kinds of checks sit behind a claim:

- Checks in the repository (`zig build check`) show glowcap agrees with itself:
  scenarios, fixtures, budgets, and public examples.
- Recurring live checks talk to the upstream OCapN suite and to a
  [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) vat. A Chromium browser
  running the WebAssembly build also connects over a local secure WebSocket.
  They run on pushes to `main` and `dev` and on pull requests that target `main`.
- On-demand checks cover Tor, a Goblins vat over iroh, and browser-to-browser
  WebRTC. No check covers iroh across two separate NATs or a public
  certificate.

A live Goblins session proves protocol interop, not API stability. A live iroh
endpoint proves the transport stack, not agreement with another CapTP
implementation.

These results count only when the
[checks workflow](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/actions/workflows/check.yml)
is green for the commit you are evaluating. The
[repository stability page](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/stability.md)
has the full table, including what each check does not prove.
[Community review](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/community-review.md)
is the outside-peer brief.

Use [Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) for deployment
tradeoffs and [Performance]($link.page('performance')) for measured costs. The
repository keeps the complete [feature
inventory](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/shipped.md) and
[known limitations](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/boundaries.md).
